  {
    "function_v0": {
      "doc": "Step 1 of a two-step ownership handover: the current owner nominates a\\nsuccessor. Nothing moves until the nominee calls `accept_ownership`.\\n\\nThis mirrors `jwk-registry`'s `propose_owner`/`accept_ownership` and\\nexists for the same reason: the owner is the only principal that can\\nfinalize the merkle root and \\xe2\\x80\\x94 after `claim_deadline` \\xe2\\x80\\x94 sweep the\\nremaining balance via `initiate_reclaim` /\\n`execute_reclaim`. A one-step transfer to a typo'd or otherwise\\nuncontrolled address handed those rights to nobody, irreversibly, in a\\nsingle transaction. Requiring the nominee to prove control of its\\naddress first makes that unreachable.\\n\\nRe-proposing overwrites the pending nominee; the owner can abort with\\n`cancel_ownership_transfer`.",
      "name": "propose_owner",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "doc": "",
          "name": "new_owner",
          "type_": "address"
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "result": {
            "ok_type": "void",
            "error_type": "error"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "function_v0": {
      "doc": "",
      "name": "claim_deadline",
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "result": {
            "ok_type": "u64",
            "error_type": "error"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "function_v0": {
      "doc": "Step 2: sweep the remaining balance to the destination announced in\\nstep 1, once the notice period has elapsed. Recipient claims stay\\nunaffected until this lands; valid claims are paid from deposit first,\\nso only the unclaimed remainder is ever swept.\\n\\nThe deadline is deliberately NOT re-checked: `ready_at` is by\\nconstruction strictly greater than `claim_deadline`, and\\n`claim_deadline` is immutable (no setter exists).\\n\\nKNOWN, ACCEPTED: the notice is served once per ANNOUNCEMENT, not once\\nper sweep. A `NothingToReclaim` revert rolls back the `PendingReclaim`\\nremoval below, so an elapsed `ready_at` stays armed and any balance that\\narrives later can be swept in a single transaction with no fresh\\nnotice. It is not fixed here because the only fix is to expire the\\nannouncement, and that adds a new way for the escape hatch itself to\\nfail \\xe2\\x80\\x94 an owner assembling a multisig or hardware signature for a\\nbroken-verifier recovery can easily need more than a week, and a\\nrecovery path that times out is a worse defect than a",
      "name": "execute_reclaim",
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "result": {
            "ok_type": "void",
            "error_type": "error"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "function_v0": {
      "doc": "",
      "name": "pending_reclaim",
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "option": {
            "value_type": {
              "tuple": {
                "value_types": [
                  "address",
                  "u64"
                ]
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "function_v0": {
      "doc": "One-time finalization of the distribution root for campaigns deployed\\nwith a zero root. Deliberately gated on the *root* being unset, not on\\nthe balance being zero: an is-it-funded check would let anyone brick a\\ncampaign forever by sending it 1 stroop before the creator finalizes\\n(see `set_merkle_root_allows_unsolicited_dust_before_root_finalization`).\\n`deposit` already refuses to run against a zero root, so the ordering\\nthe funding check was meant to enforce is enforced from that side.",
      "name": "set_merkle_root",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "doc": "",
          "name": "merkle_root",
          "type_": {
            "bytes_n": {
              "n": 32
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "result": {
            "ok_type": "void",
            "error_type": "error"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "function_v0": {
      "doc": "Step 2: the nominated successor accepts, proving it controls the\\naddress. Only then does ownership actually move.\\n\\nAny announced sweep is dropped: an incoming owner must not inherit an\\nalready-elapsed notice window and execute in the same ledger it takes\\ncontrol. It has to re-announce and serve the window itself.",
      "name": "accept_ownership",
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "result": {
            "ok_type": "void",
            "error_type": "error"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "function_v0": {
      "doc": "Batch view over the claim guards so callers resolve a recipient's\\nwhole epoch chain in one simulation instead of one per epoch.\\n\\nOn an archived (expired-TTL) guard this does NOT read `false`: the\\nguards are *persistent* entries, so an archived one is not treated as\\nabsent. Touching it makes the invocation fail with a restore\\nrequirement \\xe2\\x80\\x94 a simulation comes back with a restore preamble, and a\\nsubmitted transaction fails until the entry is restored (or, on\\nautomatic restore, is charged for and then read at its stored value,\\n`true`). Either way an already-claimed guard can never silently\\ndegrade into \"unclaimed\": both this view and the on-chain `claim`\\nstill reject after the restore. The Zarf indexer reads guard entries\\ndirectly via getLedgerEntries; this bounded view is available to\\nindependent integrators.",
      "name": "claimed_statuses",
      "inputs": [
        {
